he exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $3x=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508976 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8a37d3e-d129-5ae7-8ba8-cbd709bbd8bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508976 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2